        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B018",
    "B049"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B018 to B046. Inflation remains a profound and observationally confronted framework, but the round-1 exchange makes the comparative distinction clear: lattice clocks have reached an independently reproduced, controlled 10^-18 regime and are already usable for fundamental tests and metrology. Katori's magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept and Ye's decisive experimental development provide a compact concept-to-realization account. This is a mature discovery-led prizeable unit, provided the citation is not allowed to absorb the broader history of optical frequency metrology.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains essential. Optical lattice clocks depend on a larger optical-clock and frequency-metrology community, so the case fails if Katori and Ye cannot be shown to delimit the specific lattice-clock achievement. Their downstream applications and any redefinition of the second should not be treated as prerequisites or as credit for the whole field.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that optical lattice clocks have demonstrated reproducible 10^-18 precision, controlled systematics, and a sharply attributable Katori-Ye concept-to-realization path, independently of a future redefinition of the second.",
        "response": "I agree. The decisive consideration is not an administrative metrological milestone but the already demonstrated capacity to make controlled frequency comparisons in a new physical regime. This directly answers my earlier reservation about whether practical adoption was sufficiently settled, while preserving the need for a narrow historical citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson maintains that quantum error correction establishes the platform-independent physical possibility of scalable quantum computation, although sustained fully fault-tolerant operation and the precise credit boundary remain unsettled.",
        "response": "The principle is exceptionally deep, but I retain the distinction between an indispensable framework and a physical achievement whose defining performance is repeatedly realized. Until sustained fault-tolerant operation and the division among codes, thresholds, and topological protection are more sharply resolved, B046 is the more mature and exactly attributable recommendation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig regards the defect-mediated theory of two-dimensional melting as a mature, tested mechanism, but identifies possible overlap with already recognized topological phase-transition physics.",
        "response": "I take that overlap seriously. It illustrates why a tightly bounded advance matters: B046 can be confined to the lattice-clock method and experimentally established precision, rather than claiming the full prior history of optical metrology. Its independent operational maturity then provides the stronger present case."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review supports credit limited to Katori and Ye for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the final citation can cleanly separate lattice clocks from the broader development of optical clocks and frequency metrology.",
      "For B018, whether present cosmological observations discriminate the inflationary quantum-perturbation framework sufficiently from viable alternatives."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
